function fs_new_content_guides_4a() {
    return [
    [
        'slug'      => 'how-much-house-can-i-afford',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-01 09:00',
        'title'     => 'How Much House Can I Afford? The 28/36 Rule Explained',
        'keyword'   => 'how much house can i afford',
        'desc'      => 'How much house can I afford? Use the 28/36 rule, today’s mortgage rates and a worked example to find a safe home price and monthly payment for your income.',
        'excerpt'   => 'The 28/36 rule, a worked example with real numbers and the costs lenders count, so you can set a home budget before you shop.',
        'answer'    => 'A common guideline is the 28/36 rule: keep your total housing payment (principal, interest, taxes, insurance and PMI) at or below 28% of gross monthly income, and all debt payments at or below 36%. At a 7.32% rate with 10% down, a $90,000 income with $700 of other debts supports a home of about $256,000.',
        'takeaways' => [
            'Housing costs at or below 28% of gross income and total debt at or below 36% is a common starting rule.',
            'Your rate, down payment, property tax and other debts move your price more than income alone.',
            'Lenders may approve more than the 28/36 rule; approval is a ceiling, not a target.',
            'Keep cash for closing costs and an emergency fund on top of the down payment.',
        ],
        'content'   => '<p class="lead"><strong>How much house can I afford?</strong> For most buyers the honest answer starts with one number: the monthly payment you can carry without strain. Lenders and planners often use the <strong>28/36 rule</strong> to set that limit, then work backward to a home price using the mortgage rate, your down payment, property taxes and insurance. This guide explains the rule, walks through a worked example with real numbers, and shows what changes the result. To run your own numbers, open the <a href="{tool:loan-affordability-calculator}">loan affordability calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is the 28/36 rule?</h2><p>The 28/36 rule is a debt-to-income guideline. It says your <strong>housing payment</strong> should be no more than <strong>28% of your gross monthly income</strong>, and your <strong>total monthly debt payments</strong>, including the new housing payment, should be no more than <strong>36%</strong>. Gross income means pay before taxes and deductions.</p><p>The housing payment is often called PITI: principal, interest, property taxes and homeowners insurance. If you put less than 20% down on a conventional loan, private mortgage insurance (PMI) is added, and HOA dues count too. Total debt adds car loans, student loans, minimum credit card payments, personal loans and child support. Everyday costs such as groceries, utilities and phone bills are not counted, which is one reason the rule can feel generous in real life.</p><p>The rule is a rule of thumb, not a law. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau defines your <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-debt-to-income-ratio-en-1791/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">debt-to-income ratio</a> as all monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income and notes that different loan products and lenders set different limits. Fannie Mae’s <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-02/debt-income-ratios" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Selling Guide</a>, for example, sets a 36% maximum total ratio for manually underwritten loans, which can go up to 45% with strong credit and reserves, and allows up to 50% for loans run through its automated system. So a lender may approve you for more than 28/36 allows. That approval is the most you can borrow, not the amount that keeps your budget comfortable.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed in late 2026</h2><ul><li><strong>Rates eased slightly.</strong> According to <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac</a>, the average 30-year fixed rate was 7.40% on October 8 and October 15, 2026, and fell to 7.32% on October 22, 2026. A year earlier it was 6.19%, so buyers today need more income for the same home than in late 2025. Rates change weekly, so check the latest figure before you shop.</li><li><strong>PMI and FHA insurance are deductible again for 2026.</strong> The IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">draft 2026 Publication 936</a> says the itemized deduction for qualified mortgage insurance premiums has been reinstated for tax year 2026, following P.L. 119-21 (the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, enacted July 4, 2025). The deduction shrinks once AGI passes $100,000 and disappears above $109,000 ($50,000 and $54,500 if married filing separately). The publication is still a draft, so confirm the final version.</li><li><strong>2027 loan limits are not out yet.</strong> The <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/conforming-loan-limit" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FHFA</a> updates conforming loan limits at the end of each November, effective January 1. Until then the 2026 baseline of $832,750 applies.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How do you calculate how much house you can afford?</h2><ol><li><strong>Find your gross monthly income.</strong> Divide annual income before taxes by 12.</li><li><strong>Apply 28%.</strong> Multiply monthly income by 0.28. This is your housing cap.</li><li><strong>Apply 36% and subtract your debts.</strong> Multiply monthly income by 0.36, then subtract existing monthly debt payments. This is your second cap.</li><li><strong>Use the lower of the two.</strong> That is the most you should spend on PITI plus PMI and HOA.</li><li><strong>Turn the payment into a price.</strong> Take out taxes, insurance and PMI, and the rest pays principal and interest. The standard formula M = P × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>) converts a payment into a loan amount, and the loan plus your down payment is the price.</li></ol><p>Step 5 is where a calculator helps, because taxes and PMI depend on the price you are solving for. The <a href="{tool:mortgage-calculator}">mortgage calculator</a> shows the full monthly payment for any price, and the <a href="{tool:debt-to-income-ratio-calculator}">debt-to-income ratio calculator</a> checks both ratios.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: a $90,000 income</h2><p>Here is the whole calculation for one household, using these assumptions:</p><ul><li>Gross income $90,000 a year ($7,500 a month)</li><li>Existing debts: $700 a month (a car loan and a student loan)</li><li>Mortgage rate 7.32%, 30-year fixed, the Freddie Mac average as of October 22, 2026</li><li>Down payment 10%</li><li>Property tax 1.1% of the price per year, homeowners insurance 0.4% per year, PMI 0.5% of the loan per year (all assumptions; yours will differ)</li></ul><p><strong>Step 1 and 2:</strong> 28% of $7,500 is <strong>$2,100</strong>. <strong>Step 3:</strong> 36% of $7,500 is $2,700; minus $700 of debts leaves <strong>$2,000</strong>. <strong>Step 4:</strong> the lower cap is $2,000, so the debts, not the 28% limit, decide the budget.</p><p><strong>Step 5:</strong> solving for the price where the full payment equals $2,000 gives a home of about <strong>$256,000</strong>:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Home price</td><td>$256,168</td></tr><tr><td>Down payment (10%)</td><td>$25,617</td></tr><tr><td>Loan amount</td><td>$230,551</td></tr><tr><td>Principal and interest (7.32%, 30 years)</td><td>$1,583.73</td></tr><tr><td>Property tax (1.1% a year)</td><td>$234.82</td></tr><tr><td>Homeowners insurance (0.4% a year)</td><td>$85.39</td></tr><tr><td>PMI (0.5% of loan a year)</td><td>$96.06</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total monthly housing payment</strong></td><td><strong>$2,000.00</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>If the same household had no other debts, the 28% cap of $2,100 would apply and the price would rise to about <strong>$269,000</strong>. Paying off the $700 of monthly debts adds roughly $13,000 of buying power, which is why clearing a car loan before applying can matter as much as a raise.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford on my salary?</h2><p>Using the same assumptions (7.32%, 30 years, 10% down, no other debts), here is a quick guide. The full table from $45,000 to $200,000, with a version for people who carry $500 a month of other debt, is in our <a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford-by-salary/">how much house can I afford by salary</a> guide.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Annual income</th><th>Max housing payment (28%)</th><th>Estimated home price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$45,000</td><td>$1,050</td><td>About $134,000</td></tr><tr><td>$60,000</td><td>$1,400</td><td>About $179,000</td></tr><tr><td>$70,000</td><td>$1,633</td><td>About $209,000</td></tr><tr><td>$100,000</td><td>$2,333</td><td>About $299,000</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>I make $70,000 a year: how much house can I afford?</h3><p>At $70,000 your gross monthly income is about $5,833, so the 28% cap is about $1,633 a month. With the assumptions above that supports a home of roughly $209,000 with about $20,900 down. A lower rate, a bigger down payment or a low-tax area raises that figure.</p><h3>I make $45,000 a year: how much house can I afford?</h3><p>At $45,000 the 28% cap is $1,050 a month, which supports about $134,000 at today’s average rate. With $500 of other monthly debts the 36% cap becomes binding and the price falls to about $109,000. Programs with lower down payments, such as FHA, can help with cash but do not change the payment math much.</p><h3>If I make 100k a year, how much house can I afford?</h3><p>At $100,000 the 28% cap is about $2,333 a month, which supports roughly $299,000 with 10% down, or about $346,000 with 20% down because PMI drops away and the loan is a smaller share of the price.</p>'
            . '<h2>What else changes how much you can afford?</h2><h3>Mortgage rate</h3><p>Rates move the price more than most buyers expect. Freddie Mac’s <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Primary Mortgage Market Survey</a> put the average 30-year fixed rate at 7.32% and the 15-year at 6.68% as of October 22, 2026. Rates change weekly, and the rate you are offered depends on your credit, down payment and loan type. Improving your score before applying can lower it; see <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">how to improve your credit score fast</a>. Some buyers pay points to lower the rate, which we cover in <a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">mortgage points explained</a>.</p><h3>Down payment</h3><p>A larger down payment lowers the loan and, on conventional loans, can remove PMI at 20%. It also means more cash at closing, so balance it against your emergency fund.</p><h3>Property taxes and insurance</h3><p>A 1.1% tax rate on a $300,000 home is $275 a month; in a 2% county it is $500. Check the real rate with the <a href="{tool:property-tax-calculator}">property tax calculator</a> and get an insurance quote before you commit.</p><h3>HOA dues and upkeep</h3><p>HOA dues count in the housing payment. Maintenance does not, but it is real: many owners budget around 1% of the home’s value a year for repairs.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford with a VA or FHA loan?</h2><p>Loan type mainly changes the cash you need and the insurance you pay, not the income math.</p><ul><li><strong>VA loans:</strong> According to <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">VA.gov</a>, eligible service members and veterans can buy with no down payment as long as the price is not higher than the appraised value, with no PMI, though a one-time VA funding fee may apply. With the $2,100 payment cap from the example and no down payment, the same assumptions support about $259,000, before any funding fee. Use the <a href="{tool:va-loan-calculator}">VA loan calculator</a> and see our <a href="/va-loan-benefits-complete-guide-veterans-2026/">VA loan benefits guide</a>.</li><li><strong>FHA loans:</strong> The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/fha-loans/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB</a> notes FHA loans allow down payments as low as 3.5%, and mortgage insurance is required on all FHA loans. HUD’s Mortgagee Letter 2026-11 (October 8, 2026) keeps 580 as the minimum decision credit score for maximum financing. Try the <a href="{tool:fha-loan-calculator}">FHA loan calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Conventional loans:</strong> For 2026 the FHFA set the baseline conforming loan limit for a one-unit home at <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/news/news-release/fhfa-announces-conforming-loan-limit-values-for-2026" rel="noopener" target="_blank">$832,750</a>, with a ceiling of $1,249,125 in high-cost areas. Larger loans are jumbo loans with their own rules.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How do other rules compare?</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What it limits</th><th>Based on</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>28/36 rule</td><td>Housing at or below 28%, all debt at or below 36%</td><td>Gross income</td></tr><tr><td>Dave Ramsey guideline</td><td>Housing payment at or below 25%, on a 15-year fixed loan</td><td>Take-home pay</td></tr><tr><td>Lender automated approval</td><td>Total debt can reach 45% to 50% on some loans</td><td>Gross income</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On his own site, <a href="https://www.ramseysolutions.com/real-estate/how-much-house-can-i-afford" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Dave Ramsey</a> recommends a housing payment of no more than 25% of take-home pay on a 15-year fixed loan, including taxes, insurance, PMI and HOA. That is much stricter than 28/36: a 15-year loan has a higher payment, and take-home pay is lower than gross pay. Online tools, including those on large real estate sites such as Zillow, generally ask for the same inputs (income, debts, down payment, rate) and apply a debt-to-income limit, so results differ mainly in the assumptions they use.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much can I afford on a house without being house poor?</h2><p>Being “house poor” means the payment fits the ratio but leaves little room for anything else. A few checks help:</p><ul><li><strong>Build the budget from take-home pay.</strong> Put the full housing payment into the <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a> and see what is left. The <a href="/50-30-20-budget-rule-guide-2026/">50/30/20 budget rule</a> is a useful cross-check.</li><li><strong>Keep reserves.</strong> After the down payment and closing costs you still want an emergency fund. See <a href="/emergency-fund-how-much-where-to-keep-2026/">how much emergency fund you need</a> and the <a href="{tool:emergency-fund-calculator}">emergency fund calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Plan the cash.</strong> Closing costs are separate from the down payment. A <a href="{tool:savings-goal-calculator}">savings goal calculator</a> shows how long it takes to save both.</li><li><strong>Test a higher rate.</strong> If you might refinance later, do not count on it. Check the payment at today’s rate.</li></ul><p>First-time buyers can find the full step-by-step process in our <a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">first-time home buyer guide</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford-by-salary/">How much house can I afford by salary ($45K to $200K)</a></li><li><a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">What are points on a mortgage, and are they worth it?</a></li><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">Should I refinance my mortgage? A break-even guide</a></li><li><a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">First-time home buyer guide 2026</a></li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac PMMS</a> (rates as of October 22, 2026); <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-debt-to-income-ratio-en-1791/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB on debt-to-income ratio</a> and <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/fha-loans/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FHA loans</a>; <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-02/debt-income-ratios" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fannie Mae Selling Guide B3-6-02</a>; <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/news/news-release/fhfa-announces-conforming-loan-limit-values-for-2026" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FHFA 2026 conforming loan limits</a>; <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">VA purchase loans</a>; HUD Mortgagee Letter 2026-11. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS draft 2026 Publication 936</a> (mortgage insurance premium deduction). Example figures are our own calculations with the assumptions stated.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'      => [
            [ 'How much house can I afford with the 28/36 rule?', 'Multiply gross monthly income by 0.28 for your housing cap, and by 0.36 minus your other debts for a second cap. The lower number is your maximum payment for principal, interest, taxes, insurance and PMI. A calculator then turns that payment into a home price using your rate and down payment.' ],
            [ 'I make $70,000 a year, how much house can I afford?', 'At $70,000 the 28% cap is about $1,633 a month. With a 7.32% 30-year rate, 10% down, 1.1% property tax, 0.4% insurance and PMI, that supports a home of about $209,000. Other debts, a different rate or a larger down payment will change the result.' ],
            [ 'I make $45,000 a year, how much house can I afford?', 'At $45,000 the 28% cap is $1,050 a month, which supports a home of about $134,000 with the same assumptions. If you also pay $500 a month on other debts, the 36% cap becomes the limit and the estimate falls to roughly $109,000.' ],
            [ 'What home price fits a $100,000 salary?', 'With $100,000 of income and no other debts, the 28% cap is about $2,333 a month. At 7.32% with 10% down that supports roughly $299,000, or about $346,000 with 20% down, because PMI drops away and the loan is a smaller share of the price.' ],
            [ 'Does a VA loan let me buy a more expensive home?', 'VA loans can require no down payment if the price is not above the appraised value, and they have no PMI, though a funding fee may apply. With a $2,100 payment cap and our assumptions, that supports about $259,000. The income ratios still apply, so check your debts first.' ],
            [ 'What is the Dave Ramsey rule for how much house I can afford?', 'Dave Ramsey recommends a total housing payment of no more than 25% of take-home pay on a 15-year fixed-rate loan, including taxes, insurance, PMI and HOA dues. Because it uses take-home pay and a shorter loan, it gives a much lower price than the 28/36 rule.' ],
            [ 'How much can I afford on a house if lenders approve me for more?', 'Lenders can approve total debt ratios of 45% to 50% on some loans, which is well above 36%. Approval shows the most you can borrow, not what fits your budget. Check the payment against your take-home pay, savings goals and an emergency fund before choosing a price.' ],
            [ 'Is the Zillow affordability calculator accurate?', 'Affordability calculators on real estate sites, including Zillow, use similar inputs: income, debts, down payment, rate, taxes and insurance. Results differ mainly because of the assumptions behind them. Enter your real property tax rate, insurance quote and current rate offer to get a figure you can rely on.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'mortgage-points-explained',
        'title'      => 'What Are Points on a Mortgage? Are They Worth It?',
        'keyword'    => 'what are points on a mortgage',
        'desc'       => 'What are points on a mortgage? Learn how discount points work, how to find your break-even point with a worked example, and when points are tax deductible.',
        'excerpt'    => 'Mortgage points trade cash at closing for a lower rate; here is how to tell whether that trade pays off for you.',
        'answer'     => 'Points on a mortgage, also called discount points, are upfront fees you pay at closing to lower your interest rate. One point costs 1% of the loan amount. They pay off only if you keep the loan past the break-even point: the cost of the points divided by the monthly payment savings.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'One discount point costs 1% of the loan amount; the rate cut it buys varies by lender.',
            'Break-even months equal the cost of points divided by the monthly savings.',
            'Points usually make sense only if you expect to keep the loan well past break-even.',
            'Points on a main-home purchase can be deductible in the year paid if IRS tests are met; refinance points are spread over the loan.',
        ],
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-02 09:00',
        'content'    => '<p class="lead"><strong>What are points on a mortgage?</strong> Points, or discount points, are fees you pay the lender at closing in exchange for a lower interest rate. You pay more now to pay less every month. Whether that trade is worth it depends on how long you keep the loan, and this guide shows how to work it out with a real break-even example, how points appear on your loan documents, and when the IRS lets you deduct them. To compare payments, use the <a href="{tool:mortgage-calculator}">mortgage calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:break-even-calculator}">break-even calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What are points on a mortgage?</h2><p>Mortgage points are prepaid interest. According to the <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-should-i-use-lender-credits-and-points-also-called-discount-points-en-136/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau</a>, <strong>one point equals one percent of the loan amount</strong>, so one point on a $300,000 loan costs $3,000. You can often buy a fraction of a point, such as 0.5 or 0.375.</p><p>There is no fixed rate cut per point. The CFPB says the reduction depends on the lender, the type of loan and market conditions. Its own example shows 0.375 points ($675) on a $180,000 loan lowering the rate from 5.00% to 4.875%. Many lenders quote something near a quarter of a percentage point per point, but you should treat that only as a starting assumption and compare actual quotes.</p><h3>What do points mean on a mortgage Loan Estimate?</h3><p>Points are listed on page 2, Section A (origination charges) of your Loan Estimate and Closing Disclosure. Some lenders also charge an origination fee that is sometimes expressed in points. That fee pays for processing the loan and does not lower your rate, so check which line you are looking at.</p><h3>Discount points vs lender credits</h3><p>Lender credits work the other way. The lender gives you money toward closing costs in exchange for a higher rate. They appear as a negative number on page 2, Section J. A credit is sometimes described as negative points. Credits can make sense if you are short on cash or expect to sell or refinance soon.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed in late 2026</h2><ul><li><strong>Rates dipped this month.</strong> <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac</a> reported a 30-year fixed average of 7.32% on October 22, 2026, down from 7.40% on October 8 and 15. That is still well above the 6.19% of a year earlier. If rates keep falling, a future refinance becomes more likely, which shortens the time you keep the loan and works against buying points.</li><li><strong>Mortgage insurance is deductible again for 2026.</strong> The IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">draft 2026 Publication 936</a> lists one change under What’s New: the itemized deduction for qualified mortgage insurance premiums is reinstated for tax year 2026 under P.L. 119-21, with a phaseout above $100,000 of AGI and no deduction above $109,000. The same draft keeps the $750,000 limit on home acquisition debt. If you are weighing points against a larger down payment that would remove PMI, this changes the after-tax math for some itemizers.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How do mortgage points work? A break-even example</h2><p>Here is a full worked example. Assumptions:</p><ul><li>Loan amount $360,000, 30-year fixed</li><li>Rate with no points: 7.32% (the Freddie Mac average for a 30-year fixed loan as of October 22, 2026)</li><li>Assumed offer: each point lowers the rate by 0.25 percentage points (actual offers vary)</li></ul><p>The monthly payment uses the standard amortization formula M = P × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>), where r is the monthly rate and n is 360 payments.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Rate</th><th>Upfront cost</th><th>Monthly principal and interest</th><th>Monthly savings</th><th>Break-even</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No points</td><td>7.32%</td><td>$0</td><td>$2,472.95</td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><td>1 point</td><td>7.07%</td><td>$3,600</td><td>$2,412.04</td><td>$60.91</td><td>About 59 months</td></tr><tr><td>2 points</td><td>6.82%</td><td>$7,200</td><td>$2,351.73</td><td>$121.22</td><td>About 59 months</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Break-even formula:</strong> cost of points ÷ monthly savings. For one point that is $3,600 ÷ $60.91 = 59.1 months, or just under five years. If you sell or refinance before then, you lose money on the points. If you keep the loan longer, you come out ahead.</p><h3>What you gain or lose over time</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Years you keep the loan</th><th>Net result, 1 point</th><th>Net result, 2 points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3 years</td><td>−$1,407</td><td>−$2,836</td></tr><tr><td>5 years</td><td>+$55</td><td>+$73</td></tr><tr><td>10 years</td><td>+$3,710</td><td>+$7,347</td></tr><tr><td>30 years</td><td>+$18,329</td><td>+$36,440</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures compare payment savings with the upfront cost and ignore taxes and what the cash could earn elsewhere. Because the lower rate also pays the balance down a little faster, the simple break-even slightly understates the benefit: after five years the 1-point loan’s balance is about $881 lower than the no-points loan. On the other hand, if the $3,600 could earn interest in savings, the true break-even is a little later. The simple formula is a good first test.</p>'
            . '<h2>Is it a good idea to buy points on a mortgage?</h2><p>Points tend to make sense when:</p><ul><li><strong>You plan to stay a long time.</strong> The CFPB notes that points are generally a better fit if you keep the loan for a long time. Ask yourself whether you are confident you will keep this exact loan well past the break-even date.</li><li><strong>You are unlikely to refinance.</strong> If rates fall and you refinance, the old loan ends and so do the savings from its points. If you think rates may drop, read <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">should I refinance my mortgage</a> first.</li><li><strong>You have cash left after closing.</strong> Money spent on points cannot go to your down payment, emergency fund or moving costs. A larger down payment can sometimes save more, especially if it gets you to 20% and removes PMI.</li><li><strong>The offer is good.</strong> If a point buys only a small rate cut, the break-even stretches out. Compare quotes from several lenders.</li></ul><p>Points tend not to make sense if you may move within a few years, expect to refinance, or would have to drain savings to pay them. In some cases a seller or builder offers to pay points as a concession, which changes the math because the cash is not yours.</p><h3>Points vs a temporary buydown</h3><p>A temporary buydown (such as a 2-1 buydown) lowers the payment for the first one or two years only, then the rate returns to the note rate. Discount points lower the rate for the life of the loan. They solve different problems: a buydown eases the early years, while points reduce long-term cost.</p>'
            . '<h2>Are mortgage points tax deductible?</h2><p>Because points are prepaid interest, they can be deductible if you itemize. The rules come from <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc504" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 504</a> and <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p936" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 936</a>.</p><h3>When points are deductible in the year you pay them</h3><p>You can deduct the full amount in the year paid if you itemize on Schedule A, use the cash method of accounting (as most individuals do), and all of these are true:</p><ol><li>The loan is used to buy, build or improve your main home.</li><li>Your main home secures the loan.</li><li>Paying points is an established practice where the loan was made.</li><li>The points are not more than the amount generally charged in that area.</li><li>You provided funds at or before closing at least equal to the points, not borrowed from the lender or broker. Points the seller pays are treated as paid by you if you reduce your home’s basis by that amount.</li><li>The points are computed as a percentage of the loan principal.</li><li>The settlement statement clearly shows the amount as points.</li></ol><h3>When points must be spread over the loan</h3><ul><li><strong>Refinance points</strong> are generally deducted over the life of the loan. Publication 936 gives an example: two points of prepaid interest ($2,000) on a 15-year refinance, with six payments made in the year, give a first-year deduction of $67 ($2,000 ÷ 180 months × 6).</li><li><strong>Second-home points</strong> also must be deducted over the life of the loan.</li><li>Fees that are not interest, such as appraisal, notary and loan preparation fees, are not deductible as interest, even if they are labeled as points.</li></ul><p>Two more limits matter. The deduction only helps if your itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction. And for loans taken out after December 15, 2017, Publication 936 (2025) limits deductible home acquisition debt to $750,000, or $375,000 if married filing separately. A tax professional can confirm how the rules apply to you.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to compare offers with and without points</h2><ol><li><strong>Ask each lender for two quotes</strong> on the same day: one with no points and one with points. The CFPB recommends comparing options side by side.</li><li><strong>Check page 2, Section A</strong> of each Loan Estimate to see exactly what you pay for points.</li><li><strong>Calculate the monthly savings</strong> with the <a href="{tool:mortgage-calculator}">mortgage calculator</a> or the <a href="{tool:amortization-calculator}">amortization calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Divide cost by savings</strong> to get break-even months, or use the <a href="{tool:break-even-calculator}">break-even calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>Compare with your plans.</strong> If break-even is five years and you expect to move in four, skip the points.</li></ol><p>Before you decide on points, make sure the overall price fits your budget. Our hub guide <a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford/">how much house can I afford</a> explains the 28/36 rule, and the <a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">first-time home buyer guide</a> covers closing costs. A better credit score can also lower your rate without any upfront cost; see <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">how to improve your credit score fast</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>When do mortgage points not pay off?</h2><p>Take the same $360,000 loan and suppose the buyer pays one point, then sells the home after three years. They paid $3,600 at closing and saved $60.91 a month for 36 months, or $2,193. The net result is a loss of about $1,407. With two points the loss doubles to about $2,836. The same thing happens if rates fall and the buyer refinances in year three: the new loan replaces the old one, and the remaining savings from the points disappear. Points are a bet that you will keep this specific loan for a long time, so the decision depends more on your plans than on the rate itself.</p><h3>Mortgage points calculator: what to enter</h3><p>A mortgage points calculator needs only a few inputs, all of which are on your Loan Estimate:</p><ul><li>The loan amount and term</li><li>The rate without points and the rate with points, from quotes issued on the same day</li><li>The number of points, or their dollar cost from page 2, Section A</li><li>How many years you expect to keep the loan</li></ul><p>It returns the monthly savings, the break-even month and the net gain or loss at the end of the period you entered. You can do the same with our <a href="{tool:mortgage-calculator}">mortgage calculator</a> by running each rate, subtracting the payments, and dividing the cost of the points by the difference.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes with mortgage points</h2><ul><li><strong>Comparing quotes from different days.</strong> Rates change, so a quote with points from Monday and one without points from Friday are not comparable.</li><li><strong>Confusing origination fees with discount points.</strong> Only discount points lower the rate.</li><li><strong>Ignoring the chance of refinancing.</strong> If you refinance after two years, points on the original loan are mostly wasted.</li><li><strong>Leaving no cash cushion.</strong> Keep an emergency fund after closing; see <a href="/emergency-fund-how-much-where-to-keep-2026/">how much emergency fund you need</a>.</li></ul>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-should-i-use-lender-credits-and-points-also-called-discount-points-en-136/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB, lender credits and discount points</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc504" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 504, home mortgage points</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p936" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Publication 936 (2025)</a>; <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac PMMS</a> (rate as of October 22, 2026). <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS draft 2026 Publication 936</a> (mortgage insurance premium deduction). Payment and break-even figures are our own calculations with the assumptions stated.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What are points on a mortgage?', 'Points, or discount points, are upfront fees paid to the lender at closing to lower your interest rate. One point equals 1% of the loan amount, so one point on a $300,000 loan costs $3,000. They are a form of prepaid interest and appear on page 2, Section A of your Loan Estimate.' ],
            [ 'How do mortgage points work?', 'You pay a percentage of the loan at closing, and the lender lowers your rate for the life of the loan. The size of the cut is not fixed and varies by lender and market. Your monthly payment drops, and after enough months the savings cover the upfront cost.' ],
            [ 'Is it a good idea to buy points on a mortgage?', 'It can be if you expect to keep the loan well past the break-even point, are unlikely to refinance soon, and still have savings after closing. If you may move or refinance within a few years, the upfront cost usually outweighs the monthly savings.' ],
            [ 'How do I calculate the break-even on mortgage points?', 'Divide the cost of the points by the monthly payment savings. In our example, one point on a $360,000 loan costs $3,600 and saves $60.91 a month, so break-even is about 59 months, just under five years. A mortgage points or break-even calculator does this for you.' ],
            [ 'What do points mean on a mortgage quote?', 'On a quote or Loan Estimate, points show how much you pay upfront for the quoted rate. Positive points cost you money and lower the rate; lender credits, sometimes called negative points, reduce closing costs but raise the rate. Origination charges listed as points do not lower the rate.' ],
            [ 'Are discount points on a mortgage tax deductible?', 'They can be. Points on a loan to buy or build your main home may be deducted in full in the year paid if you itemize and meet the IRS tests in Topic 504. Refinance and second-home points are generally deducted over the life of the loan.' ],
            [ 'Can the seller pay my mortgage points?', 'Yes, sellers sometimes pay points as a concession. The IRS treats seller-paid points as paid by you for the deduction tests if you reduce your home’s basis by that amount. The seller cannot deduct them as interest; they are treated as a selling expense instead.' ],
            [ 'Should I buy points or make a bigger down payment?', 'Compare both. A larger down payment lowers the loan and can remove PMI at 20% down, while points lower the rate. Run each option in a mortgage calculator and compare total cost over the years you expect to keep the loan, keeping enough cash for emergencies.' ],
        ],
