    [
        'slug'       => 'how-much-house-can-i-afford-by-salary',
        'title'      => 'How Much House Can I Afford With 200k Salary? $45K to $200K',
        'keyword'    => 'how much house can i afford with 200k salary',
        'desc'       => 'How much house can I afford with a 200k salary, or $45k to $150k? See a 28/36 rule table by income with today’s rate, taxes, insurance and a debt variant.',
        'excerpt'    => 'A salary-by-salary table from $45,000 to $200,000 using the 28/36 rule, with every assumption shown.',
        'answer'     => 'Using the 28/36 rule, a 7.32% 30-year rate, 10% down, 1.1% property tax, 0.4% insurance and PMI, a $200,000 salary supports a home of about $598,000 with a $4,667 monthly payment. A $100,000 salary supports about $299,000 and a $60,000 salary about $179,000. Other debts lower these figures.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Under 28/36, housing costs should stay at or below 28% of gross monthly income.',
            'At a 7.32% rate with 10% down, a $200,000 salary supports roughly a $598,000 home.',
            'With $500 a month of other debt, incomes under $75,000 can afford noticeably less.',
            'A one-point rate change moves the affordable price by about 7% to 8%.',
        ],
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-04 09:00',
        'content'    => '<p class="lead"><strong>How much house can I afford with 200k salary?</strong> Using the 28/36 rule and current average mortgage rates, a $200,000 income supports a home price of about <strong>$598,000</strong> with 10% down. This guide gives the same estimate for every income from $45,000 to $200,000, shows exactly how each number was calculated, and explains what changes it. For your own figures, use the <a href="{tool:loan-affordability-calculator}">loan affordability calculator</a>. For the method itself, see our hub guide <a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford/">how much house can I afford</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What assumptions are behind these numbers?</h2><p>Every home price below comes from the same set of assumptions. Change any of them and the price changes, so treat the table as a starting point:</p><ul><li><strong>Rule:</strong> 28/36. Total housing payment at or below 28% of gross monthly income; all debts including housing at or below 36%.</li><li><strong>Rate:</strong> 7.32%, 30-year fixed. That is the Freddie Mac <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Primary Mortgage Market Survey</a> average as of October 22, 2026. Rates change weekly and your offer depends on credit and loan type.</li><li><strong>Down payment:</strong> 10% of the price.</li><li><strong>Property tax:</strong> 1.1% of the price per year (an assumption; local rates vary widely).</li><li><strong>Homeowners insurance:</strong> 0.4% of the price per year (an assumption).</li><li><strong>PMI:</strong> 0.5% of the loan per year, because the down payment is under 20% (an assumption).</li><li><strong>No HOA dues.</strong></li></ul><p>The loan payment uses the standard amortization formula M = P × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>), with r the monthly rate and n = 360. We solved for the price where principal, interest, tax, insurance and PMI together equal the payment cap.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford by salary? The full table</h2><p>The first price column assumes no other monthly debts, so the 28% housing cap applies. The second assumes $500 a month of other debts, such as a car loan, so the 36% cap (36% of income minus $500) applies when it is lower.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Annual salary</th><th>Gross monthly income</th><th>Max housing payment (28%)</th><th>Home price, no other debt</th><th>Down payment (10%)</th><th>Home price, $500/month other debt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$45,000</td><td>$3,750</td><td>$1,050</td><td>$134,000</td><td>$13,400</td><td>$109,000</td></tr><tr><td>$50,000</td><td>$4,167</td><td>$1,167</td><td>$149,000</td><td>$14,900</td><td>$128,000</td></tr><tr><td>$60,000</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$1,400</td><td>$179,000</td><td>$17,900</td><td>$167,000</td></tr><tr><td>$70,000</td><td>$5,833</td><td>$1,633</td><td>$209,000</td><td>$20,900</td><td>$205,000</td></tr><tr><td>$75,000</td><td>$6,250</td><td>$1,750</td><td>$224,000</td><td>$22,400</td><td>$224,000</td></tr><tr><td>$80,000</td><td>$6,667</td><td>$1,867</td><td>$239,000</td><td>$23,900</td><td>$239,000</td></tr><tr><td>$100,000</td><td>$8,333</td><td>$2,333</td><td>$299,000</td><td>$29,900</td><td>$299,000</td></tr><tr><td>$120,000</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$2,800</td><td>$359,000</td><td>$35,900</td><td>$359,000</td></tr><tr><td>$150,000</td><td>$12,500</td><td>$3,500</td><td>$448,000</td><td>$44,800</td><td>$448,000</td></tr><tr><td>$200,000</td><td>$16,667</td><td>$4,667</td><td>$598,000</td><td>$59,800</td><td>$598,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Prices are rounded to the nearest $1,000. Why does the $500 debt column match the first column from $75,000 up? The gap between the 36% and 28% caps is 8% of income. At $75,000 that is exactly $500 a month, so for higher incomes a $500 debt still fits under 36% and the 28% cap remains the limit. Larger debts would lower those prices too. Check your own ratio with the <a href="{tool:debt-to-income-ratio-calculator}">debt-to-income ratio calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Latest update: what changed in late 2026</h2><ul><li><strong>Rates edged down.</strong> <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac</a> put the 30-year fixed average at 7.32% on October 22, 2026, down from 7.40% the two previous weeks but up from 6.19% a year earlier. Every figure in the table uses 7.32%; see the rate table below to adjust for newer weeks.</li><li><strong>PMI deduction is back for 2026.</strong> The IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">draft 2026 Publication 936</a> says qualified mortgage insurance premiums are deductible again for tax year 2026 under P.L. 119-21 if you itemize. The deduction phases out between $100,000 and $109,000 of AGI, so it mainly helps buyers in the $45,000 to $100,000 rows above who put less than 20% down.</li><li><strong>2027 loan limits expected in late November.</strong> The <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/data/conforming-loan-limit" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FHFA</a> announces new conforming loan limits at the end of November. None of the loans in the table come close to the 2026 baseline of $832,750.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: what does a $100,000 salary buy?</h2><p>Here is the full breakdown at $100,000 a year with no other debts. Gross monthly income is $8,333.33, so the 28% cap is $2,333.33.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Home price</td><td>$298,863</td></tr><tr><td>Down payment (10%)</td><td>$29,886</td></tr><tr><td>Loan amount</td><td>$268,976</td></tr><tr><td>Principal and interest (7.32%, 30 years)</td><td>$1,847.68</td></tr><tr><td>Property tax (1.1%)</td><td>$273.96</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance (0.4%)</td><td>$99.62</td></tr><tr><td>PMI (0.5% of loan)</td><td>$112.07</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total monthly payment</strong></td><td><strong>$2,333.33</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Notice that only about $1,848 of the $2,333 goes to the loan itself. The same $2,333 payment would buy noticeably less in a county with a 2% property tax rate and noticeably more in one with a 0.6% rate, which is why two buyers with identical salaries can get very different answers. Before you rely on any figure in this guide, replace our tax and insurance assumptions with real numbers for the area where you plan to buy. Taxes, insurance and PMI take about a fifth of the budget, which is why the same salary buys more in a low-tax area.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford with 200k salary, 150k or 120k?</h2><h3>How much house can I afford with 200k salary?</h3><p>At $200,000 your gross monthly income is $16,667, and 28% is about <strong>$4,667</strong> a month. Under our assumptions that supports about <strong>$598,000</strong>, with roughly $59,800 down and a loan near $538,000. That loan is below the FHFA’s 2026 baseline conforming loan limit of <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/news/news-release/fhfa-announces-conforming-loan-limit-values-for-2026" rel="noopener" target="_blank">$832,750</a> for a one-unit home, so it would not need to be a jumbo loan. In high-cost areas, where the limit can reach $1,249,125, buyers at this income often face higher prices and may need a larger down payment.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 150k salary?</h3><p>At $150,000 the 28% cap is $3,500 a month, which supports about <strong>$448,000</strong> with about $44,800 down. With 20% down the same payment stretches further because PMI drops away.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 120k salary?</h3><p>At $120,000 the cap is $2,800 a month and the estimate is about <strong>$359,000</strong>. A two-income household earning $120,000 combined uses the same math; lenders add both incomes and both sets of debts.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford with 100k, 80k or 75k salary?</h2><h3>How much house can I afford with 100k salary?</h3><p>About <strong>$299,000</strong> with 10% down, as in the worked example, or about $346,000 with 20% down.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 80k salary?</h3><p>At $80,000 the 28% cap is about $1,867 a month, supporting a home of about <strong>$239,000</strong> with $23,900 down. A $500 debt does not change this figure, but a $700 debt would, because it would push total debt above 36%.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 75k salary?</h3><p>At $75,000 the cap is $1,750 a month and the estimate is about <strong>$224,000</strong>. This is the point where a $500 monthly debt exactly uses up the room between the two caps.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much house can I afford with 70k, 60k or 50k salary?</h2><h3>How much house can I afford with 70k salary?</h3><p>At $70,000 the 28% cap is about $1,633 a month, supporting about <strong>$209,000</strong>. With $500 of other debts the 36% cap is $1,600 and the price falls slightly to about $205,000.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 60k salary?</h3><p>At $60,000 the cap is $1,400 a month, supporting about <strong>$179,000</strong>, or about $167,000 with $500 of other debts.</p><h3>How much house can I afford with 50k salary?</h3><p>At $50,000 the cap is about $1,167 a month and the estimate is about <strong>$149,000</strong>. Debts matter most at this level: with $500 a month of other payments the estimate falls to about $128,000. At $45,000 the figures are $134,000 and $109,000. Paying down a car loan or credit card balance before applying can raise your budget more than a small raise would.</p><p>Buyers at these incomes often look at low-down-payment loans. The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/fha-loans/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB</a> notes FHA loans allow down payments as low as 3.5%, with mortgage insurance required, and <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">VA.gov</a> says eligible veterans can buy with no down payment and no PMI, though a funding fee may apply. Compare them with the <a href="{tool:fha-loan-calculator}">FHA loan calculator</a> and the <a href="{tool:va-loan-calculator}">VA loan calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Does 20% down or a 15-year loan change the answer?</h2><p>Yes, in opposite directions. A 20% down payment removes PMI and makes the loan a smaller share of the price, so the same monthly payment supports a more expensive home. The catch is the cash: you need twice as much up front, plus closing costs.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Annual salary</th><th>Home price, 10% down</th><th>Home price, 20% down</th><th>Cash for 20% down</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$60,000</td><td>$179,000</td><td>$208,000</td><td>$41,500</td></tr><tr><td>$100,000</td><td>$299,000</td><td>$346,000</td><td>$69,200</td></tr><tr><td>$150,000</td><td>$448,000</td><td>$519,000</td><td>$103,800</td></tr><tr><td>$200,000</td><td>$598,000</td><td>$692,000</td><td>$138,400</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Same assumptions as above, with no PMI in the 20% column. A 15-year loan works the other way. Freddie Mac put the average 15-year fixed rate at 6.68% as of October 22, 2026. At that rate, with 10% down and the same tax, insurance and PMI assumptions, a $100,000 salary supports about $244,000 instead of $299,000, because the loan is repaid in half the time. The reward is far less interest: on a $268,976 loan, total interest is about $396,000 over 30 years at 7.32% but about $158,000 over 15 years at 6.68%. The <a href="{tool:amortization-calculator}">amortization calculator</a> shows the year-by-year difference.</p>'
            . '<h2>How do mortgage rates change these numbers?</h2><p>Here is the estimated price for a $100,000 salary (a $2,333 payment cap, 10% down, same tax, insurance and PMI assumptions) at different rates:</p><table><thead><tr><th>30-year rate</th><th>Estimated home price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6.00%</td><td>$332,000</td></tr><tr><td>6.50%</td><td>$319,000</td></tr><tr><td>7.00%</td><td>$307,000</td></tr><tr><td>7.32%</td><td>$299,000</td></tr><tr><td>8.00%</td><td>$284,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each percentage point moves the affordable price by roughly 7% to 8% at this income: from 6% to 7% the estimate falls by about $25,000. Some buyers pay discount points to lower the rate; whether that pays off depends on how long you keep the loan, which we explain in <a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">mortgage points explained</a>. A higher credit score can lower your rate without upfront cost; see <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">how to improve your credit score fast</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What should you check before using these numbers?</h2><ul><li><strong>Your real property tax rate.</strong> Our 1.1% assumption can be far off. Look up yours with the <a href="{tool:property-tax-calculator}">property tax calculator</a>.</li><li><strong>An insurance quote.</strong> Premiums vary a lot by state and by risks such as storms and wildfire.</li><li><strong>HOA dues.</strong> Every $100 of monthly dues lowers the affordable price by roughly $13,000 at today’s rate in our model.</li><li><strong>Cash for closing.</strong> You need the down payment plus closing costs plus an emergency fund. See <a href="/emergency-fund-how-much-where-to-keep-2026/">how much emergency fund you need</a>.</li><li><strong>Your take-home budget.</strong> The 28/36 rule uses gross pay. Plug the payment into the <a href="{tool:monthly-budget-planner}">monthly budget planner</a> to see what remains after taxes and other costs, or compare with the <a href="/50-30-20-budget-rule-guide-2026/">50/30/20 budget rule</a>.</li></ul><p>For the full home-buying process, read the <a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">first-time home buyer guide</a>. If you already own and want to lower your payment, see <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">should I refinance my mortgage</a>.</p>'
            . '<p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.freddiemac.com/pmms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Freddie Mac PMMS</a> (30-year fixed average as of October 22, 2026); <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/news/news-release/fhfa-announces-conforming-loan-limit-values-for-2026" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FHFA 2026 conforming loan limits</a>; <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/fha-loans/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">CFPB on FHA loans</a>; <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">VA purchase loans</a>. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/p936--dft.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS draft 2026 Publication 936</a> (mortgage insurance premium deduction). All home prices are our own calculations with the assumptions stated above.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 200k salary?', 'With a $200,000 salary and no other debts, the 28% rule allows about $4,667 a month for housing. At a 7.32% 30-year rate with 10% down, 1.1% property tax, 0.4% insurance and PMI, that supports a home of about $598,000. Local taxes and your actual rate will change it.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 150k salary?', 'At $150,000 a year the 28% cap is $3,500 a month. Using the same assumptions, that supports a home of roughly $448,000 with about $44,800 down. A 20% down payment removes PMI and raises the price you can afford with the same monthly payment.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 100k salary?', 'A $100,000 salary gives a 28% housing cap of about $2,333 a month. At 7.32% with 10% down that supports about $299,000, or about $346,000 with 20% down. At a 6% rate the 10%-down figure rises to about $332,000, which shows how much the rate matters at this income.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 80k salary?', 'At $80,000 the 28% cap is about $1,867 a month, which supports a home of about $239,000 with 10% down under our assumptions. Other debts up to about $533 a month do not lower this, but larger debts push you past the 36% total limit.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 75k salary?', 'At $75,000 the 28% cap is $1,750 a month, supporting about $224,000 with 10% down at a 7.32% rate. At this income $500 of other monthly debt exactly fills the gap between the 28% and 36% limits, so any more debt lowers the price.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 60k salary?', 'At $60,000 the 28% cap is $1,400 a month, supporting a home of about $179,000 with 10% down. If you also pay $500 a month on other debts, the 36% limit applies and the estimate falls to about $167,000. Checking your real property tax rate can move the figure as well.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 50k salary?', 'At $50,000 the 28% cap is about $1,167 a month, which supports roughly $149,000 under our assumptions. With $500 of monthly debts the estimate drops to about $128,000. FHA loans with 3.5% down can reduce the cash needed, though mortgage insurance applies.' ],
            [ 'How much house can I afford with 120k salary?', 'At $120,000 the 28% cap is $2,800 a month, which supports a home of about $359,000 with 10% down at a 7.32% rate. Couples with a combined $120,000 income use the same math, counting both incomes and all monthly debts.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
